+            zero, and the output pass stops one iteration short of writing past the
+            real product range. Reads the tail ulong directly from
+            <paramref name="x"/> / <paramref name="y"/> and synthesises the
+            half-V128 tail in registers via <c>Vector128.Create(tail, 0)</c>.
+            </remarks>
+        </member>
+        <member name="T:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.Large">
+            <summary>
+            Sealed <see cref="T:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.IBinPolyMul"/> impl selected for sizes at or above the PCLMUL
+            <see cref="F:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.Large.KaratsubaCutoff"/>. Implements multiplication via a Karatsuba recursion
+            that bottoms out via <see cref="M:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.Large.ImplLeaf(System.ReadOnlySpan{System.UInt64},System.ReadOnlySpan{System.UInt64},System.Span{System.UInt64})"/> into the uniform <c>ImplMulEven</c> /
+            <c>ImplMulOdd</c> arbitrary-degree-Karatsuba leaf (by even/odd length). The class
+            name reflects the dispatch criterion (large operand size); the algorithm inside is
+            Karatsuba.
+            </summary>
+            <remarks>
+            Internally <see cref="T:System.Span`1"/>-based: the public array+offset <see cref="M:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.Large.Multiply(System.UInt64[],System.Int32,System.UInt64[],System.Int32,System.UInt64[],System.Int32)"/>
+            entry converts to <see cref="T:System.ReadOnlySpan`1"/> / <see cref="T:System.Span`1"/> at the top of
+            the call and the recursion uses <c>Span.Slice(...)</c> throughout. The CLMUL-only TFM
+            guard (<c>NETCOREAPP3_0_OR_GREATER</c>) makes this safe — Spans are always available
+            where this class compiles. The recursion's scratch buffer is allocated per
+            <see cref="M:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.Large.Multiply(System.UInt64[],System.Int32,System.UInt64[],System.Int32,System.UInt64[],System.Int32)"/> call and threaded through as a parameter, so instances are
+            safe to use concurrently.
+            </remarks>
+        </member>
+        <member name="M:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.Large.KaratsubaScratchSize(System.Int32)">
+            <summary>
+            Words of scratch space the top-level <see cref="M:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.Large.Multiply(System.UInt64[],System.Int32,System.UInt64[],System.Int32,System.UInt64[],System.Int32)"/> entry must allocate
+            for an <see cref="M:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.Large.ImplKaratsuba(System.ReadOnlySpan{System.UInt64},System.ReadOnlySpan{System.UInt64},System.Span{System.UInt64},System.Span{System.UInt64})"/> call over an operand of length
+            <paramref name="len"/>. Computed by walking the halving chain from
+            <paramref name="len"/> down to the cutoff: each level contributes
+            <c>2 * ceil(len/2)</c> for that frame's <c>zMid</c> output slot. The
+            <c>ta</c> / <c>tb</c> halving-sums reuse the parent's <c>zz</c> output region
+            (pre-overwrite by <c>z0</c> / <c>z2</c>), so they need no scratch of their
+            own. Frames stack consecutively from offset 0 upward — each parent claims the
+            first <c>2m</c> words of its scratch view and hands the suffix
+            (<c>scratch.Slice(2m)</c>) to its child sub-calls. Name keeps Karatsuba because
+            the scratch layout is intrinsic to the Karatsuba recombination shape.
+            </summary>
+        </member>
+        <member name="M:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.Large.ImplLeaf(System.ReadOnlySpan{System.UInt64},System.ReadOnlySpan{System.UInt64},System.Span{System.UInt64})">
+            <summary>
+            Leaf dispatch for the Karatsuba recursion base case (<c>x.Length &lt;
+            KaratsubaCutoff</c>). Even lengths go through <c>ImplMulEven</c>, odd
+            lengths through <c>ImplMulOdd</c> -- both arbitrary-degree Karatsuba
+            over <c>Vector128&lt;ulong&gt;</c> limbs, with the odd kernel handling
+            the half-V128 tail in registers.
+            </summary>
+            <remarks>
+            Reachable lengths here are the Karatsuba leaf range <c>[16, 31]</c> (one halving
+            below the PCLMUL cutoff of 32). All sub-cutoff top-level sizes route to other
+            impls (<see cref="T:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.Size1"/>..<see cref="T:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.Size10"/>, <see cref="T:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.MediumEven"/>,
+            or <see cref="T:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.MediumOdd"/>) and never reach this leaf. The even/odd split
+            here mirrors the <see cref="T:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.MediumEven"/> / <see cref="T:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.MediumOdd"/> split
+            at the top-level dispatch and calls the same kernels.
+            </remarks>
+        </member>
+        <member name="M:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.Large.ImplKaratsuba(System.ReadOnlySpan{System.UInt64},System.ReadOnlySpan{System.UInt64},System.Span{System.UInt64},System.Span{System.UInt64})">
+            <summary>
+            Recursive Karatsuba multiply (Span-based). Writes
+            <c>x[0..x.Length-1] * y[0..x.Length-1]</c> (carryless) into
+            <c>zz[0..2*x.Length-1]</c>, overwriting prior contents. The
+            <paramref name="scratch"/> span is sized once at the entry call
+            (<see cref="M:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.Large.KaratsubaScratchSize(System.Int32)"/> for <c>m_size</c>); each frame parks its
+            <c>zMid</c> output at <c>scratch[0..2m)</c> and recurses with
+            <c>scratch.Slice(2m)</c>, so frames stack disjointly along the descent. The
+            <c>ta</c> / <c>tb</c> halving-sums are written into the parent's <c>zz</c>
+            region (where <c>z0</c> / <c>z2</c> will eventually live) and are overwritten
+            by the <c>z0</c> / <c>z2</c> sub-calls. Sub-product slots are always
+            overwritten by the recursive calls, so the caller need not pre-zero
+            <paramref name="scratch"/>.
+            </summary>
+            <remarks>
+            <para>The recombine reads every limb of the <c>zMid</c> output
+            (<c>scratch[0..2m)</c>), so the <c>zMid</c> sub-call must write every output
+            limb. Both leaves do (<c>ImplMulEven</c> writes every V128 lane;
+            <c>ImplMulOdd</c> writes every lane within its <c>2*len</c>-ulong contract,
+            with the tail-V128 zero-fill handled implicitly via the diagonal phase's
+            single PCLMULQDQ). A future leaf that elides a slack-only limb would have
+            to either restrict the contract to "writes all limbs" or add per-frame
+            defensive zeroing here.</para>
+            </remarks>
+        </member>
+        <member name="T:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.MediumEven">
+            <summary>
+            Sealed <see cref="T:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.IBinPolyMul"/> impl for the even-sized half of the mid-band:
+            dispatched for even <c>m_size</c> values in
+            <c>[12, Large.KaratsubaCutoff)</c> that don't have a dedicated per-size impl
+            (<see cref="T:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.Size1"/>..<see cref="T:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.Size10"/>). Counterpart to
+            <see cref="T:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.MediumOdd"/>, which handles odd <c>m_size</c> in the same range.
+            </summary>
+            <remarks>
+            Multiply is a single stackalloc + <see cref="M:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.Kernels.ImplMulEven(System.ReadOnlySpan{System.UInt64},System.ReadOnlySpan{System.UInt64},System.Span{System.UInt64})"/> + reduce.
+            The kernel views the operand ulong spans as <c>Vector128&lt;ulong&gt;</c> "limbs"
+            (each V128 = 2 packed ulongs) and runs a flat arbitrary-degree single-level
+            Karatsuba: <c>L = m_size / 2</c> diagonal sub-multiplies plus <c>L*(L-1)/2</c>
+            cross-product sub-multiplies, each a <c>Mul2x2</c> on V128 limbs. Direct analog
+            of the scalar arbitrary-degree kernel. The upper bound
+            (<see cref="F:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.Large.KaratsubaCutoff"/>) is where the O(L^2) flat structure loses to
+            the recursive Karatsuba descent in <see cref="T:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.Large"/>.
+            </remarks>
+        </member>
+        <member name="T:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.MediumOdd">
+            <summary>
+            Sealed <see cref="T:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.IBinPolyMul"/> impl for the odd-sized half of the mid-band:
+            dispatched for odd <c>m_size</c> values in
+            <c>[11, Large.KaratsubaCutoff)</c>. Counterpart to <see cref="T:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.MediumEven"/>,
+            which handles even <c>m_size</c> in the same range.
+            </summary>
+            <remarks>
+            Multiply is a single stackalloc tt + <see cref="M:Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BinPoly.X86.V128.Kernels.ImplMulOdd(System.ReadOnlySpan{System.UInt64},System.ReadOnlySpan{System.UInt64},System.Span{System.UInt64})"/> + reduce
